    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old woman presented with a 6-month history of progressive dysarthria and dysphagia. Family history notable for a relative with memory problems after age 80.\n\nNeurological examination revealed:- Cognitive impairment- Vertical gaze palsy - Tongue atrophy- Pyramidal signs\n\n{'Imaging Studies': '11C-CFT PET imaging of brain showed:- Severe loss of dopamine transporter binding in basal ganglia- Almost total loss in caudate nucleus- DAT binding measurements:  - Left caudate: 0.35 (normal 1.55-2.07)  - Right caudate: 0.36 (normal 1.55-2.07)  - Left anterior putamen: 0.43 (normal 1.91-2.53)  - Right anterior putamen: 0.53 (normal 1.91-2.53)  - Left posterior putamen: 0.64 (normal 1.58-2.01)  - Right posterior putamen: 0.81 (normal 1.58-2.01)Image Title: 11C-CFT PET in Patient and Healthy ParticipantImage Description: Decreased 11C-CFT binding in bilateral basal ganglia, with caudate most severely impaired', 'Electrophysiological Studies': 'Needle EMG showed:- Large-amplitude, long-duration motor unit potential in frontalis, mentalis, orbicularis oculi, and genioglossus muscles- Unremarkable nerve conduction studies and EMG in other regions', 'Laboratory Tests': '- Autoimmune encephalitis antibodies negative in serum and CSF- Genetic testing: Heterozygous pathogenic variant of TBK1 gene (p.E643del) identified by whole-exon sequencing'}",
